USE_CASES_MD = """
# Use Cases for Automated PR Review

## Why Code Review Automation Matters

Large engineering organizations process **thousands of pull requests per day**.
At that scale, human reviewers become a bottleneck: context switching is costly,
security gaps are missed under time pressure, and review quality becomes
inconsistent across teams.

Manual review averages **4–6 hours to first response** at median-size companies.
Security vulnerabilities introduced in PRs sit undetected for days. Junior
engineers receive terse, unhelpful feedback. Senior engineers spend 30% of their
week on code review.

---

## Current Pain Points

| Pain point | Impact |
|---|---|
| Review bottleneck | Slows velocity, blocks releases |
| Inconsistent standards | Some reviewers strict, others permissive |
| Security gaps | SQL injection, secrets in code slip through |
| Config blind spots | Dockerfile, YAML, workflows often under-reviewed |
| No triage for OSS projects | Community PRs queue for weeks |

---

## Our Approach vs Alternatives

### Static Analysis Tools (Semgrep, Pylint, Bandit)
- **Pro:** Fast, deterministic, language-specific
- **Con:** No cross-file reasoning; no PR-level context; no config awareness
- **Our edge:** We aggregate multiple tools and reason over the whole diff

### LLM One-Shot Review (GPT-4, Claude)
- **Pro:** Understands intent; natural language feedback
- **Con:** Expensive per PR; non-deterministic; no structured evidence trail
- **Our edge:** Small model (1.7B) + structured tool calls + reward shaping

### Our RL Agent Approach
- Learns *which tools to call* given the diff and file types
- Efficient: 3-5 tool calls beats a 10-second full scan
- Produces structured evidence before emitting a verdict
- Trainable on your own task bank — adapts to your codebase

---

## Real-World Scenarios

### Scenario 1: Startup — 2 Engineers, No Dedicated Reviewer
> Two-person team shipping fast. Neither engineer has time for thorough review.

Our agent acts as a **first-pass reviewer**: runs security and quality checks
automatically on every PR, surfaces the top 3 concerns, and recommends
approve / request-changes. Engineers only review the flagged items.

**Result:** 80% of routine PRs approved without human review time.

---

### Scenario 2: Enterprise — Security Compliance Gate
> Regulated industry. Every PR touching auth/infra must pass a security check
> before reaching human reviewers.

Our agent runs `check_security` and `check_config` on every PR to the
`infra/` and `auth/` directories. Rejections block the merge button.
Human reviewers only see pre-screened PRs.

**Result:** Security issues caught at submission, not post-merge.

---

### Scenario 3: OSS Project — Automated Community PR Triage
> Popular open-source project with 50+ external contributors per week.
> Maintainers are overwhelmed.

Our agent labels community PRs as `needs-tests`, `security-concern`, or
`ready-for-review` within 60 seconds of submission. Maintainers prioritize
`ready-for-review` PRs first.

**Result:** Maintainer review time reduced by 40%; contributor wait time halved.

---

### Scenario 4: CI/CD Integration — Block Merges on Failing Checks
> Every PR runs the agent in CI. Merges blocked until security + config pass.

```yaml
# .github/workflows/pr-review.yml
- name: PR Review Agent
  run: python -m benchmarks.run_baselines --episodes 1
  env:
    PR_REVIEW_TOOL_BACKEND: heuristic
```

**Result:** Consistent gate across all PRs; no manual configuration per team.

---

## Supported Languages and File Types

**Code languages:**
Python, TypeScript, JavaScript, Java, Go, Rust

**Repo-global files:**
Dockerfile, YAML (including Kubernetes manifests), GitHub Actions workflows, `.gitignore`

**Task families:**
- Pure code PRs
- Pure config / infra PRs
- Mixed PRs (code + config in the same diff)

---

## The Efficiency Argument

A full semgrep + pylint + type-check scan can take 10-30 seconds per PR.

Our RL agent learns to call only the **3-5 tools most relevant** to the diff:
- Python code? → `check_security` + `check_quality` + `check_tests`
- Dockerfile change? → `check_config` + `check_security`
- Go refactor? → `check_build_and_types` + `check_quality`

**Average latency: under 2 seconds in heuristic mode.**

The reward function penalises redundant tool calls, teaching the agent to be
concise and decisive — just like a great senior engineer.
"""

